I’ll inspect the diffraction data and metadata first, then identify candidate phases, refine them jointly against the pattern, and verify that the exported CIFs, profile, fractions, and GSAS-II project all agree. I’ll keep the phase count data-driven and call out any ambiguity in the final report.

The chemistry strongly points to the PbO–WO₃ reaction system, but at 500 °C several lead tungstate stoichiometries and residual oxides are plausible. I’m now mapping the peak positions and checking the installed phase-search/refinement utilities before committing to candidates.

The peak sets separate cleanly into three chemically sensible patterns: scheelite PbWO₄ dominates; monoclinic WO₃ accounts for the 23–25° triplet and related lines; orthorhombic PbO (massicot) accounts for the 29.3/30.6° pair and its higher-angle series. I’m testing those three structures together now, including the Cu Kα doublet and the angle-dependent specimen-displacement shift visible across the scan.

The first joint refinement is converging, and all three phase scales are positive and significant. The initial profile fit is intentionally conservative; I’m now tightening the zero offset, unit cells, and crystallite broadening, then I’ll compare alternate WO₃ and lead-tungstate models rather than accepting the first chemically plausible combination.

The chemically correct reference structures are now established, but the absolute fractions move by a few percentage points when unconstrained line-broadening terms are allowed—an expected consequence of having no calibrated instrument profile. I’ve therefore switched to a consistent constrained profile model and am comparing (in parallel) the three-phase solution against vacancy-ordered lead tungstate, litharge PbO, and two-phase omissions.

The comparison jobs are still refining rather than waiting on external services. The key test is whether the extra minor-phase peak families disappear when PbO or WO₃ is omitted; I’m also checking that the vacancy-ordered Pb-deficient model does not gain fit quality merely from its larger cell and extra reflections.

The model-selection result is decisive: the three-phase model gives \(R_{wp}=14.68\%\), versus 15.98% without WO₃ and 17.17% without PbO; litharge is driven essentially to zero. Vacancy-ordered Pb₇W₈O₂₈.₈ is also worse (15.31%), so the dominant phase is ordinary scheelite PbWO₄. I’m now exporting the converged, physically constrained model and checking every deliverable against the saved final project.

The exports are written. I’m doing the final integrity pass now: reopening `final.gpx`, cross-checking its phase fractions and calculated profile against `result.json`/`fit.csv`, and independently parsing each exported CIF to verify formula, symmetry, and refined cell.

